<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="eigen-functions-complete">
    <title>Eigenfunctions (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Now that we have some basics of eigenvalues and eigenvectors, we'll look at some specific examples
                        from quantum mechanics.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>The Position Operator</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                Recall, the position operator is <m>\hat{x}: V \to V</m>, <m>\hat{x}[\psi] \mapsto x \cdot \psi</m>.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        The eigen-problem is then
                        <md>
                            \hat{x}[\psi] = x_0 \psi
                        </md>.
                        Eigenvectors to problems in this context of function spaces are called <term>eigenfunctions</term>. 
                        This seems like an issue: how can <m>x\psi(x)</m> be the same as a constant times <m>\psi(x)</m>
                        for every <m>x \in \R</m>?
                        
                    </p>
                    <p>
                        The intuition here is that every <m>x_0 \in \R</m> should be a possible observed outcome for the position
                        of a particle. So for <m>x_0\in \R</m>, we need eigenfunctions whose entire support is at <m>x_0</m>, 
                        and <m>0</m> everywhere else. For example, if <m>x_0 =0</m>, the idea is something like
                        <md>
                        \psi(x) = \begin{cases}
                            0 \amp \text{ if } x \neq 0 \\
                            \infty \amp \text{ if } x =0
                        \end{cases}
                        </md>
                        Such a <q>function</q> would capture <m>x \psi(x) = 0 \cdot \psi(x)</m>.

                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="8in">
        <title>The Dirac Delta <q>Function</q></title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Eigenfunctions of the position operator are <term>Dirac Delta <q>Functions</q></term>. We can construct them as follows.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Consider a collection of functions of the form <m>g_t(x) = \frac{1}{t\sqrt{\pi}}e^{-x^2/t^2}</m>.
                See the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/poqo3g9l1o">Dirac Delta Desmos calculator</url> 
                for a visual of these functions.
                
            </p>
            <p>
                The <term>Dirac Delta function</term> <m>\delta(x)</m> is defined as <m>\lim_{t \to 0} g_t(x)</m>, which as seen
                in the visual satisfies the intuition
                <md>
                    \delta(x) = \begin{cases}
                            0 \amp \text{ if } x \neq 0 \\
                            \infty \amp \text{ if } x =0
                        \end{cases}
                </md>,
                and more generally
                <md>
                    \delta(x-c) = \begin{cases}
                            0 \amp \text{ if } x \neq c \\
                            \infty \amp \text{ if } x =c
                        \end{cases}
                </md>
                
            </p>
            <p>
                The defining properties of the dirac delta are how it behaves under integration:
                <ol>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^\infty \delta (x) \,\, dx = 1</m> and <m>\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^\infty \delta (x-c) \,\, dx = 1</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^\infty f(x) \delta (x-c) \,\, dx = f(c)</m>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The set of functions <m>\{\psi_{x_0}\}_{x_0 \in \R}</m>, where <m>\psi_{x_0} = \delta(x-x_0)</m> is 
                            an orthogonal set in the following sense:
                            <md>
                                <mrow>\langle \psi_a, \psi _b \rangle \amp= \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{\psi}_a \cdot \psi_b \,\, dx </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \int_{-\infty}^\infty \delta(x-a) \cdot \delta(x-b) \,\, dx </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \delta(a-b) </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp=  \begin{cases}
                            0 \amp \text{ if } a \neq b \\
                            \infty \amp \text{ if } a =b
                        \end{cases}</mrow>
                            </md>.
                            Note that this differs from our previous notion of orthogonality only in that the inner product of a function with itself is not finite.
                            Obtaining functions satisfying this notion of orthogonality is called <term>delta function normalization</term>.
                            
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
</page>
<page>
    <exercise>
        <title>The Momentum Operator</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall, the momentum operator is <md>\hat{p}: V \to V, \,\, \hat{p}[\psi] \mapsto -i \overline{h}\cdot \frac{d}{dx}[ \psi ]</md>.
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                The eigen problem here is 
                <md>
                    -i\overline{h} \frac{d}{dx}[\psi] = p \psi
                </md>,
                which is an example of a <term>differential equation</term> (more soon). We can verify that the eigenfunctions are 
                <m>\psi_p(x) = A e^{ipx/\overline{h}}</m>, with eigenvalue <m>p \in \R</m>.
                
            </p>
            <p>
                We can't normalize these functions in the traditional sense, but we can delta function normalize them:
                <md>
                    <mrow> \langle \psi_p, \psi_{p'}\rangle \amp= \int_{-\infty}^\infty \overline{\psi_p} \cdot \psi_{p'} \,\, dx </mrow>
                    <mrow> \amp=|A|^2 \cdot 2\pi \overline{h}\delta(p'-p) </mrow>
                </md>.
                The last equality uses facts of dirac delta that are beyond the scope of our course, but we can use it delta function 
                normalize the eigenfunctions of the momentum operator. That is,
                <md>
                    \langle A e^{ipx/\overline{h}}, A e^{ip'x/\overline{h}} \rangle = \delta(p'-p)
                </md>
                when <m>A = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \overline{h}}}</m>, so the delta function normalized eigenfunctions of <m>\hat{p}</m> are
                <m>\psi_p(x) =\frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi \overline{h}}} \cdot e^{ipx/\overline{h}} </m> for <m>p \in \R</m>.
                
                
            </p>
        </solution>
        
    </exercise>
</page>


       
    </worksheet>
